      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is an incredibly helpful process for students to learn how cells divide to grow and repair tissue. To understand the mechanics, we must first explore Prophase, where chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes and the nuclear envelope breaks down so spindle fibers can form. Next, during Metaphase, chromosomes align face-to-face at the cell's equator, a critical step that helps the cell understand proper alignment before separation. In Anaphase, the sister chromatids finally separate as spindle fibers pull them toward opposite poles, ensuring each side receives the full genetic code. Finally, Telophase completes the journey as new nuclear envelopes form around the DNA, allowing students to see how learning about these phases reveals the precise order of cell division. This structured explanation is designed to be helpful as a study guide, ensuring learners truly understand every stage from start to finish.",
